{"id":"reflect-and-develop","title":"Reflect and develop","minutes":6,"tracks":["coach"],"summary":"Use a specific moment, the child’s voice and mentor feedback to shape your next session.","sections":[{"heading":"Recall a moment","html":"<p>Reflection begins with what actually happened. Choose one short moment involving an individual child rather than rating the entire session as good or bad. Describe the game, what the child attempted, what you did and what happened next. Separate what you saw from what you inferred. ‘She stood outside the area for two restarts’ is an observation; ‘she did not care’ is an interpretation that needs checking.</p><p>Keep the child at the centre of the review. Your aim is to understand their experience and improve your next response, rather than defend the plan or find a perfect explanation.</p>"},{"heading":"Ask and listen","html":"<p>The guide's reflection sheet offers questions about favourite moments, decisions, challenges and what would help next time. Choose one question a child can answer in their own way. ‘What made you smile?’ may open a different conversation from ‘What would help you get involved?’</p><p>Give time for an answer, accept a demonstration or a brief response, and avoid turning the conversation into a test. Their answer is one perspective alongside your observation. If it surprises you, stay curious: the same game can feel different to the child and the adult.</p>"},{"heading":"Bring evidence to a mentor","html":"<p>Share a specific example with your mentor: ‘I gave a restart headstart; the child looked up on two restarts and asked for another turn.’ Ask what they noticed and whether another explanation is possible. Use an anonymous description without names or identifying personal details in the hub and use approved safeguarding and communication procedures.</p><p>A mentor conversation can help you choose a manageable next step. It cannot turn a quiz score or written reflection into proof of on-field competence. Practical observation and feedback remain necessary.</p>"},{"heading":"Choose and revisit one action","html":"<p>Decide what you will keep, change or check next session. For example, offer the same start and watch whether the child chooses space without your prompt. A successful attempt today is encouraging; revisit it in another situation before drawing broader conclusions.</p><p>Use intent over outcome in your own learning too: explain the purpose behind your coaching decision, examine its effect honestly, and stay willing to change. Record the next action plainly so your mentor can follow up.</p>"}],"mediaIds":["reflection"],"quiz":[{"id":"reflect-and-develop-1","prompt":"Your note says “He is lazy.” What would improve it?","options":["Repeat the label.","Replace it with a specific observation and ask about the child’s experience.","Use only the final score."],"correct":1,"explanation":"An observable moment can be explored. A fixed label hides uncertainty."},{"id":"reflect-and-develop-2","prompt":"A quiz is complete. What demonstrates practical application?","options":["The quiz alone proves competence.","The lesson timer is enough.","An observed coaching task, reflection and mentor feedback."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Knowledge checks support learning. On-field practice and feedback examine how a coach responds."},{"id":"reflect-and-develop-3","prompt":"An adaptation looked useful once. What should your next action include?","options":["Revisit it and check the child’s choices in another situation.","Declare it universally proven.","Stop observing."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A short observation informs the next step. It does not show retained learning."}],"takeaway":"Bring one observed moment, one child response and one next action to your mentor.","sourceRefs":[{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":33},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":39}]}
SCYS_JSON
